WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:17.638
In the last videos, we got to know discrete random variables and their distributions, for example the binomial distribution and the Poisson distribution. In this video, we move on to a continuous distribution that plays a central role in many applications: the standard normal distribution.

2
00:00:19.338 --> 00:00:22.225
The learning goals of this video are:

3
00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:33.158
First: to explain why, for a continuous random variable, probabilities are represented as areas under the density function, and why every single value has probability zero.

4
00:00:33.558 --> 00:00:42.294
Second: to describe the shape, the expected value and the standard deviation of the standard normal distribution and to show them on the density function.

5
00:00:42.694 --> 00:00:53.964
And third: to determine probabilities with the table in the formula sheet and with the R function pnorm, including exceedances, intervals and negative values.

6
00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:02.854
First, a short recap of video two: there are two types of random variables, discrete and continuous.

7
00:01:03.354 --> 00:01:08.131
For a discrete random variable, we can list in advance which values it can take.

8
00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:16.621
A continuous random variable, on the other hand, takes values with any number of decimal places. Whole ranges of the number line are possible.

9
00:01:17.221 --> 00:01:30.598
In the previous videos, we got to know three discrete random variables with their probability mass functions: the sum of two dice, the number of IT requests per day with a binomial distribution, and the number of calls per hour with a Poisson distribution.

10
00:01:31.148 --> 00:01:44.840
In each case, the x-axis shows the possible realisations, for example the sum or the number of calls. Each realisation gets its own probability, namely the height of its bar. All bar heights together add up to one.

11
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:56.581
Now we come to a continuous random variable. As an example, we take a weather app. It forecasts a temperature of eighteen degrees for tomorrow.

12
00:01:57.081 --> 00:02:03.862
The next day, we measure very precisely how warm it actually is, for example nineteen point three seven degrees.

13
00:02:04.362 --> 00:02:11.253
The forecast error is the measured minus the forecast temperature. Here it is therefore plus one point three seven degrees.

14
00:02:11.853 --> 00:02:21.838
Even with a well-functioning app, there are deviations upwards on some days and downwards on others. Small errors occur often, large errors rather rarely.

15
00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:36.335
From the ex ante perspective, we model this forecast error as a random variable Z, measured in degrees Celsius. As the measurement is very precise, Z is a continuous random variable.

16
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:59.858
We assume that the forecast error follows the so-called standard normal distribution. Z is the usual letter for a random variable that follows the standard normal distribution; this distribution is written as N zero one. For continuous random variables, a so-called density function describes how the probabilities are distributed over the possible values.

17
00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:09.335
As with the probability mass function, the x-axis shows the possible values, here the forecast errors in degrees Celsius.

18
00:03:09.835 --> 00:03:15.268
The curve is highest at zero: forecast errors close to zero occur often.

19
00:03:15.768 --> 00:03:22.403
Towards the outside, it falls symmetrically on both sides. Large errors upwards or downwards are therefore rare.

20
00:03:23.003 --> 00:03:30.060
We show the formula of the density function here only for completeness. We do not need it for our calculations.

21
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:42.225
For a discrete random variable, we could go through the realisations one by one and assign a probability to each of them. For a continuous random variable, we proceed differently.

22
00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:58.626
Every exact value, such as a forecast error of exactly one degree, that is one point zero zero zero and so on, is taken only with probability zero. This is because the possible realisations with infinitely many decimal places lie densely packed on the number line.

23
00:03:59.326 --> 00:04:07.720
Instead, probabilities are represented by areas under the density function. The height of the curve itself is therefore not a probability.

24
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:20.169
The total area under the density function is one, which corresponds to one hundred percent. This matches the fact that, for a probability mass function, all bar heights together add up to one.

25
00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:39.527
Because of the symmetry around zero, half of the area lies to the left of zero and half to the right. A forecast error below zero means that the app forecast too warm. An error above zero means that it forecast too cold. Each of the two therefore occurs with probability fifty percent.

26
00:04:40.227 --> 00:04:49.812
Because single values have probability zero, for continuous random variables it makes no difference whether we write less than or less than or equal to.

27
00:04:52.010 --> 00:05:01.109
The short notation N zero one contains two key figures of the standard normal distribution: the expected value zero and the variance one.

28
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:15.035
Because of the symmetry, the expected value lies exactly in the middle, at zero. For our example, this means: on average, the app is right. It forecasts neither systematically too warm nor too cold.

29
00:05:15.635 --> 00:05:21.247
The variance is one, and so the standard deviation, as its square root, is also one.

30
00:05:21.847 --> 00:05:34.870
Graphically, we can read off the standard deviation from the curvature of the density function. In the middle, the function is concave, it curves downwards; at the edges, it is convex, it curves upwards.

31
00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:44.679
The inflection points, where the curvature changes, lie at minus one and at one. Their distance from the middle is exactly the standard deviation of one.

32
00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:52.914
For the example, this means: the forecast errors typically vary by about one degree around the expected value of zero.

33
00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:08.209
How likely is it that the forecast error is at most one degree? We are looking for P of Z less than or equal to one, that is, the area under the density to the left of one.

34
00:06:08.809 --> 00:06:20.609
Mathematically, this area is an integral. For the density of the standard normal distribution, however, no antiderivative can be given as a formula. This is why we cannot calculate the integral by hand.

35
00:06:21.209 --> 00:06:39.508
For these areas, there is therefore a function of its own: the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution, written with the Greek letter Phi. Phi of z is the probability that Z is at most z, that is, the area to the left of z.

36
00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:48.295
The values of Phi are provided by a computer, for example R or Excel. Alternatively, we read them off the table in the formula sheet.

37
00:06:50.200 --> 00:07:01.834
We start with our question from before: how large is Phi of one, that is, the probability that the forecast error is at most one degree? Below, we see an excerpt from the table in the formula sheet.

38
00:07:02.384 --> 00:07:09.495
The row gives the digit before the decimal point and the first decimal place, the column gives the second decimal place.

39
00:07:10.045 --> 00:07:26.595
The threshold z for which we want the probability of falling below it is one, or one point zero zero. So we look in the row labelled one point zero and in the column labelled point zero zero. There we find zero point eight four one three, that is, eighty-four point one three percent.

40
00:07:27.195 --> 00:07:33.499
With a probability of just over eighty-four percent, the forecast error is therefore at most one degree.

41
00:07:34.499 --> 00:07:45.614
Second example: the probability that the forecast error is at most one point two three degrees. To do this, we split one point two three into one point two and zero point zero three.

42
00:07:46.164 --> 00:07:53.473
So we go to the row one point two and to the column point zero three and read off zero point eight nine zero seven.

43
00:07:54.073 --> 00:08:01.309
With a probability of just over eighty-nine percent, the forecast error is at most one point two three degrees.

44
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:18.219
For comparison, we calculate both values with R. In R, the cumulative distribution function of the normal distribution is called pnorm. As with pbinom and ppois, the p stands for the cumulative distribution function.

45
00:08:18.769 --> 00:08:29.586
Without further arguments, pnorm uses the standard normal distribution. With q equals one, we get zero point eight four one three and further decimal places.

46
00:08:30.186 --> 00:08:37.129
For q equals one point two three, rounded, the result is zero point eight nine zero seven.

47
00:08:37.729 --> 00:08:43.867
The results agree with the table values. The table is merely rounded to four decimal places.

48
00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:59.761
How likely is it that the actual temperature is more than one point two three degrees above the forecast? We are looking for P of Z greater than one point two three, that is, the area to the right of one point two three.

49
00:09:00.311 --> 00:09:18.596
The table does not answer this directly, as it always gives the area to the left of the threshold z. We obtain the probability we are looking for here via the complementary event: we calculate one minus the probability that the forecast error is below one point two three degrees.

50
00:09:19.196 --> 00:09:22.770
As a result, we get zero point one zero nine three.

51
00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:32.223
In R, too, we can calculate the exceedance probability via the complementary event, by calculating one minus pnorm.

52
00:09:32.773 --> 00:09:40.324
So in just under eleven percent of cases, the actual temperature is more than one point two three degrees above the forecast.

53
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:52.661
Now we are looking for the probability that the forecast error lies between one and one point two three degrees. This is the narrow area between these two values.

54
00:09:53.211 --> 00:09:59.152
To do this, we start with the area to the left of one point two three and then subtract the area to the left of one.

55
00:09:59.752 --> 00:10:03.242
As a result, we get four point nine four percent.

56
00:10:04.142 --> 00:10:08.259
In R, too, we can calculate this difference directly in one command.

57
00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:26.453
Finally, one more aspect that only concerns reading off the formula sheet. The table here starts at z equals zero. How, then, do we determine P of Z less than or equal to minus one, that is, the probability that the app forecast more than one degree too warm?

58
00:10:27.003 --> 00:10:34.513
Here, the symmetry of the density function helps: the area to the left of minus one is exactly as large as the area to the right of one.

59
00:10:35.063 --> 00:10:47.362
So both probabilities are equal. As before, we obtain the area to the right of one via the complementary event: one minus zero point eight four one three gives zero point one five eight seven.

60
00:10:48.262 --> 00:10:53.804
In general: Phi of minus z is one minus Phi of z.

61
00:10:54.804 --> 00:11:07.794
This leads to a connection with the standard deviation: with a probability of about sixty-eight percent, the forecast error lies between minus one and one, that is, within one standard deviation around the expected value.

62
00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:20.730
To sum up: for a continuous random variable, probabilities are areas under the density function. The total area is one, and every single value has probability zero.

63
00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:33.252
The standard normal distribution is symmetric around zero, with expected value zero and standard deviation one. We recognise the standard deviation by the inflection points at minus one and one.

64
00:11:33.853 --> 00:11:46.749
The cumulative distribution function Phi of z is the area to the left of z. We read off its values from the table in the formula sheet or calculate them with pnorm in R.

65
00:11:47.349 --> 00:11:56.820
We obtain exceedance probabilities via the complementary event, probabilities for intervals as a difference, and probabilities for negative values via the symmetry.

66
00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:06.757
In the next video, we get to know the general normal distribution. With it, we adapt the expected value and the standard deviation to specific applications.
